WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Ad handling at scale

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:03.030
requires a combination
of millions

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.600
of requests, rapid bids, and
serving the ads themselves.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.220
Join us as we talk
to Unity about how

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.920
they've built out their ad
system on this episode of Stack

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.431
Chat.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.260
Thanks for joining us, TJ.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.462
Tell us a little bit more
about what Unity does.

00:00:18.462 --> 00:00:19.670
THOMAS MERRITT: Thanks, Mark.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.450
Unity builds a high definition,
3D, real-time graphics

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.230
rendering engine.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.313
And it's used in game
development platforms as well

00:00:28.313 --> 00:00:29.730
as a variety of
other applications

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.940
like automotive,
architecture, and film.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.650
On the game development
side, we have

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.690
a number of back-end services
to support game publishers,

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.400
in particular the part I'm
involved in is monetization.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.300
And we have in-app purchase
services as well as

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.110
ad services.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.610
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So, yeah, I'd love

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.830
to hear more about how
the architecture actually

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.550
supports all of the ad requests
that come in for a game.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.530
THOMAS MERRITT: So
we get about tens

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.620
of thousands of ad
requests a second.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.070
So it's pretty high volume.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:02.940
And those all go into
a public GKE cluster.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.370
And that service also
makes bid requests

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.900
to third parties at the rate
of about a million a second.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.550
And those requests
are all public IP

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.820
addresses so that we can
get good performance going

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.680
to those bidders.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.770
We had tried it on Outbound Map
and that didn't work as well.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.920
Additionally, we have
a private GKE cluster

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.360
where all of our
support services

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.800
run for security reasons.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.310
And that cluster is only
accessible internally.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.285
And we also have
virtual machines

00:01:35.285 --> 00:01:37.660
that are running a variety of
databases that also provide

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.090
support for our ad network.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.580
Additionally, we
have data that's

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.000
going into Nats and
Kafka before hitting

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.000
other parts of our system.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.157
And we have log-in
going to Stock Driver.

00:01:50.157 --> 00:01:52.240
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What are
some of the other parts

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.237
of the system that
Kafka sends the data to?

00:01:55.237 --> 00:01:57.070
THOMAS MERRITT: So other
parts of the system

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:00.670
are billing, business
intelligence, and some machine

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.740
learning systems that
make training models that

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.935
are used for making decisions
at various stages within our ad

00:02:10.935 --> 00:02:11.905
network.

00:02:11.905 --> 00:02:14.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So was this
architecture built natively

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:14.970
on GCP?

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.968
Or was it migrated
from somewhere else?

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:19.260
THOMAS MERRITT: We migrated
from another cloud provider

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.820
into GCP about a year ago.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.600
And in the prior
environment, we had

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.850
a lot of services that were
on [INAUDIBLE] [? verse ?]

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:27.440
machines.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.790
We had some services
in Kubernetes.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.540
We had a variety of
different CI/CD processes.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.460
So early on in the
migration process,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.290
we did some engineering
work to figure out

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.090
how we were going to
migrate our services.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:44.760
We wanted to have a
consistent CI/CD pipeline

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.400
for all of our services in GCP.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.820
And so a lot of the
pre-work was to upgrade

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:56.250
the CI/CD pipeline for all our
services to be standardized.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.340
And that pipeline also
supported the GCP migration.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.780
Additionally, getting the ad
serving component migrated over

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.660
required solving a couple of
technical problems around DNS

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.530
and just the volume of
traffic that we have.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.030
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So now that you've

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.610
got all this up
and running in GCP,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.440
are there any changes
that you would make?

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.410
Or if you could go back, would
you do anything differently?

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.618
THOMAS MERRITT: We're pretty
happy with the structure

00:03:21.618 --> 00:03:24.810
we have in GCP right now,
being on a standardized CI/CD

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.570
pipeline has been
really helpful for us.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.320
It makes it easy to
deploy any services.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.140
We have a process
for doing that.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.230
And in particular,
changes that we are making

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.710
are migrating towards
managed database services

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.770
like [INAUDIBLE] and
dropping some of our more

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.030
customized database systems.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Sounds good.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.030
Well thank you so much
for coming in today

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:48.900
and telling us about how you
built the ad request network.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:49.440
THOMAS MERRITT:
Great to be here.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:49.940
Thanks.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.030
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Check
out this deep dive

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.340
on building an advertising
platform on Google Cloud

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.883
Platform.

00:03:58.883 --> 00:04:00.800
Thanks for watching and
make sure to subscribe

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.170
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.010
We'll see you next
time on Stack Chat.

